add('A target is recoverable exactly when it factors through the observation','ker H\\subseteq','2026-10-16','A','Linear observability',
'Which requested outputs are identifiable from a given observation, even when the entire hidden state is not?',
'The recovered kernel criterion is a complete finite-dimensional identifiability theorem. It separates state reconstruction from recovering a particular target. The distinction prevents unnecessary sensing and makes a missing-information claim testable by an explicit nullspace vector.',
['Let V,W,Z be finite-dimensional vector spaces over Q or R, with observation H:V→W and target G:V→Z.','The target is identifiable when Hx=Hy implies Gx=Gy for every x,y∈V.'],
'Kernel factorization criterion',
'The following are equivalent: the target is identifiable; ker H⊆ker G; and there is a linear L:W→Z such that G=LH. L is uniquely determined on im H but need not be unique on all W.',
'If the target is identifiable, set y=0 and x∈ker H to obtain Gx=0. Conversely, Hx=Hy means x−y∈ker H, so kernel inclusion gives Gx=Gy. To construct L on im H, define L(Hx)=Gx. If Hx=Hy the kernel condition makes this independent of the representative, and linearity follows from the linearity of H and G. Extend a basis of im H to a basis of W and define L arbitrarily on the complement. Then G=LH. The converse factorization immediately implies kernel inclusion. A violating vector v with Hv=0 but Gv≠0 is therefore a complete impossibility certificate: x and x+v have identical data and different targets.',
'Observe Hx=(x₁+x₂,x₂+x₃) and ask for Gx=x₁+2x₂+x₃.',
'Here ker H is span((1,−1,1)), and G annihilates this vector. The reconstruction is Gx=(1,1)Hx. Asking instead for x₁ fails, since its value changes along the kernel. In four dimensions, observing only the first two coordinates leaves a two-dimensional fibre; two further independent scalar linear measurements are necessary and sufficient to reconstruct the whole state, although fewer may suffice for a particular G.',
'The theorem identifies precisely which uncertainty matters for a requested mathematical output.',
'Nonlinear, noisy or restricted-state observations require revised conditions. Dimension counting is a lower bound for full linear reconstruction, not a universal lower bound for arbitrary discontinuous encodings or finite-state problems.',
'Under bounded noise, minimize an explicit norm of L subject to G=LH; the resulting operator norm bounds target error and turns exact identifiability into a conditioned sensor-design problem.',
[('Mathlib: quotient maps and linear factorization','https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Quotient/Basic.html')])
add('A moving observation obeys a closed equation exactly when its transport identity holds','moving quotients','2026-10-19','A','Time-dependent state reduction',
'How can an observation subspace move with time while still carrying an exact closed dynamical equation?',
'The recovered identity B′+BA=TB includes a term absent from a fixed quotient. It is both necessary and sufficient for exact closure on every initial state. A rotating example shows why ignoring B′ can incorrectly reject a valid reduction or accept an invalid one.',
['Let A(t),B(t),T(t) be continuous matrix functions, with B continuously differentiable. Assume x′=A(t)x and define y(t)=B(t)x(t).','All statements are on a fixed interval where the linear ODE has an invertible fundamental matrix.'],
'Exact moving-quotient criterion',
'Every solution satisfies y′=T(t)y if and only if B′(t)+B(t)A(t)=T(t)B(t) for every t. For full-row-rank B, some T exists at a given time exactly when ker B⊆ker(B′+BA).',
'The product rule gives y′=(B′+BA)x. The displayed identity therefore proves sufficiency. For necessity, fix a time t: the invertible fundamental solution maps initial states onto every possible x(t), so equality of the two proposed derivatives for all solutions forces equality of the matrices. The kernel condition is the preceding linear factorization theorem applied to B and B′+BA. When B has a right inverse R, one may construct T=(B′+BA)R, provided the kernel condition holds; it ensures that the construction acts correctly on every state. For a fixed observation B, the B′ term vanishes and the condition reduces to invariance of ker B under A.',
'Take A=[[0,−1],[1,0]] and B(t)=(cos t,sin t).',
'Here B′=(−sin t,cos t) and BA=(sin t,−cos t), so B′+BA=0. The moving scalar y is therefore constant, with T=0. Indeed x(t) is the counterclockwise rotation of x(0), and B(t) projects onto the transported first coordinate. A fixed B=(1,0) gives BA=(0,−1), which cannot equal a scalar multiple of B; the first fixed coordinate alone has no closed first-order scalar equation valid for all states.',
'The moving frame cancels the hidden rotation exactly and yields a genuine one-dimensional quotient.',
'An approximate residual B′+BA−TB gives an error term, not exact closure. Rank changes, singular frames and nonlinearity require separate treatment; no claim about the source’s unrecovered application matrices is inferred.',
'Given a residual norm bound ε(t) and a bound on hidden states, apply variation of constants to quantify reduced-output error; optimize B and T against this certified bound rather than only a fitted trajectory.',
[('NIST DLMF: systems of differential equations','https://dlmf.nist.gov/1.13')])
add('Permitted actions determine the correct finite observation quotient','four-state example required reset','2026-10-09','A','Finite-state equivalence',
'When are two hidden states indistinguishable under every permitted experiment, and how can an additional action refine that quotient?',
'The recovered future-state refinement is stronger than comparing current outputs. The relevant equivalence depends on the full action alphabet. A finite partition-refinement proof supplies an exact stopping criterion and a concrete example in which reset changes identifiability.',
['Let S be a finite state set, o:S→O an output map, and F_a:S→S deterministic actions for a in a finite alphabet.','Two states are future-equivalent if o(F_w(s))=o(F_w(t)) for every finite action word w, including the empty word.'],
'Greatest observable congruence by partition refinement',
'Start with the partition by current output and repeatedly split blocks according to the blocks containing every action successor. The process stabilizes after at most |S|−1 strict refinements, and its final blocks are precisely the future-equivalence classes.',
'At stage n, induction shows that two states share a block exactly when their outputs agree after every action word of length at most n: the next stage tests the empty word and then one action followed by a word of length at most n. Each strict refinement increases the number of nonempty blocks, which cannot exceed |S|. At a fixed point the partition respects both current output and every action, so induction over arbitrary word length gives future equivalence. Conversely states agreeing on all words are never separated at any finite stage. This proves the algorithm computes the greatest action-compatible quotient preserving observations. A new action can only refine this relation because it enlarges the set of possible experiments.',
'Use S={a,b,c,d}, outputs o(a)=o(b)=o(c)=0 and o(d)=1.',
'With only an identity action, a and b remain indistinguishable forever. Add reset with reset(a)=c, reset(b)=d, reset(c)=c and reset(d)=d. The one-letter experiment reset now separates a and b. Thus a controller that omits reset may collapse states that a richer experiment vocabulary can distinguish.',
'Identifiability is an exact property of the stated action-and-output system.',
'Current-output equality alone is insufficient. Conversely, a finite test suite proves equivalence only if it is known to include a distinguishing basis or a stabilized refinement. Continuous-state systems need additional finiteness or algebraic structure.',
'Attach a shortest distinguishing action word to every split; the resulting quotient certificate can support an adaptive experiment tree and reveal exactly which additional action reduces the unresolved state population.',
[('Hopcroft: finite-automaton minimization','https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-12-417750-5.50022-1')])
add('Worst-case experiment design has an exact finite Bellman proof','V(S)=\\min_e','2026-10-19','A','Adaptive experiment design',
'What is the minimum guaranteed measurement cost for determining a target on a finite uncertainty set?',
'The recovered decision recurrence becomes rigorous once terminal conditions, deterministic outcomes and admissible progress are specified. Its proof is an exact decision-tree induction. This gives a constructive optimum rather than an assertion that one greedy panel is best.',
['Let S be a finite set of possible states and g:S→D the target decision. An experiment e has positive cost c_e and partitions S into outcome fibres S_(e,y).','A terminal set is one on which g is constant. Ignore experiments whose only nonempty outcome is S itself; they cannot improve a worst-case exact decision.'],
'Bellman minimax recursion',
'If the available tests separate every pair with different target values, V(S)=0 on terminal sets and otherwise V(S)=min_e[c_e+max_(y:S_(e,y)≠∅)V(S_(e,y))], restricting to experiments with all nonempty fibres proper subsets. This equals the minimum worst-case cost of an exact adaptive decision tree.',
'A decision tree must choose some experiment e first. An adversarial outcome can choose the most expensive remaining fibre, so its worst-case cost is at least c_e+max_y V(S_(e,y)). Minimizing over the first choice gives a lower bound on every tree. Conversely, choose an experiment attaining the finite minimum and attach optimal subtrees on its proper fibres. Induction on |S| constructs a tree achieving exactly that cost. Pairwise separability ensures a nonterminal set has a useful splitting test and a finite exact tree; positive costs make a non-splitting first experiment wasteful. This proves both existence and optimality. For noisy observations the fibres would be replaced by feasible posterior sets and terminal robustness would need a separate definition.',
'Identify one of three states a,b,c.',
'Experiment e₁ costs one and partitions {a} from {b,c}; e₂ costs two and partitions {b} from {a,c}; a direct three-way experiment costs four. Performing e₁ first and, only if necessary, e₂ has worst-case cost three. Every one-test cheaper choice leaves a two-state ambiguity requiring the other test, so the Bellman optimum is three. The early a outcome costs only one, showing the adaptive branch structure.',
'The decision policy and its lower bound arise from the same exact recurrence.',
'A worst-case optimum need not minimize expected cost under a prior. Historical panel speedups or prediction accuracies cannot be inferred from this finite theorem without the original states, tests and evaluation data.',
'Enrich each outcome fibre with a target-separation certificate and an admissible lower bound on its remaining cost; branch-and-bound can then find an optimum while retaining a verifiable proof tree.',
[('Bellman: dynamic programming','https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400835386')])
add('Shared uncertainty should be optimized jointly in a decision difference','A=c+\\delta','2026-10-21','A','Robust mathematical decisions',
'How can a decision be certified when separate interval bounds overlap but the competing scores share the same uncertainty?',
'The recovered final October target is to certify a decision using common uncertainty and the cheapest sufficient measurement. Joint modeling preserves cancellation that is destroyed by independent interval bounds. The following theorem is deterministic and concerns abstract scores, with no empirical or financial forecast assumed.',
['Let X be a nonempty uncertainty set and let J_A,J_B:X→R be two scores, with larger preferred. Set δ(x)=J_A(x)−J_B(x).','A strict robust certificate is a positive lower bound m≤inf_(x∈X)δ(x). Attainment of the infimum is unnecessary if a bound is proved directly.'],
'Joint dominance and measurement certificates',
'If inf_X(J_A−J_B)>0, A wins for every compatible state. In the shared model J_A=c+δ, J_B=c with δ∈[7/4,9/4], the margin is at least 7/4 regardless of the range of c. Separate interval subtraction can fail to certify the same fact.',
'The first assertion is immediate from the defining inequality for every x. Its content is the order of operations: subtraction must take place at the same uncertainty state before minimization. In general inf(A−B)≥inf A−sup B, but the inequality can be strict because the separate extrema need not occur at compatible states. For c∈[0,100], separate score ranges are A∈[7/4,409/4] and B∈[0,100]; their interval difference includes −393/4, even though the exact shared difference is always positive. A new measurement with result z and error bound ε restricts X to X_z={x:|h(x)−z|≤ε}; computing the margin on X_z can only improve its lower bound because X_z⊆X. A decision-sufficient experiment is one whose every nonempty outcome fibre gives a positive margin for one of the two directions.',
'Suppose δ itself is measured with absolute error at most 1/10.',
'A result z=1/4 implies δ≥z−1/10=3/20>0, certifying A. A result z=1/20 gives δ∈[−1/20,3/20], so it does not certify either direction. With several possible tests, their costs and outcome fibres can be inserted into the finite Bellman recurrence to identify the cheapest guaranteed decision procedure.',
'The certificate exploits correlation exactly and states which remaining uncertainty matters.',
'Overlapping separate intervals do not imply an undecidable joint model, while a positive estimate without an error bound is not a robust certificate. This theorem supplies no calibration of an empirical uncertainty set.',
'Use polynomial or rational score models on a semialgebraic X and produce an exact positivity certificate for J_A−J_B; then optimize measurement cost subject to such certificates on every admissible outcome fibre.',
[('Boyd and Vandenberghe: convex optimization','https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/')])
add('Raw generator observability is an injective coding problem','M=2^{107}','2026-10-20','A','Finite arithmetic observability',
'What can be proved from the recovered generator equations, and what certificate is needed for the claimed five-output reconstruction theorem?',
'The exact raw recurrence has now been retrieved. A separately replayed all-state lattice certificate is supplied in supplemental Y26-10-901–902; the accepted-output rule remains unavailable. Pigeonhole counting gives a rigorous lower bound. A collision criterion and a completely enumerated small analogue explain what an independent injectivity proof would have to establish.',
['Let M=2¹⁰⁷−1, A=3¹²¹ mod M and b=2²⁶. States are x∈{0,…,M−1}, with F(x)=Ax mod M and output o(x)=x mod b.','The length-k observation code is C_k(x)=(o(x),o(Fx),…,o(F^(k−1)x)). Accepted/rejected observations define a different code and require their actual acceptance rule.'],
'Information lower bound and exact collision obligations',
'Four raw outputs cannot determine every state because b⁴=2¹⁰⁴<M. Five outputs have sufficient alphabet capacity but are not proved injective by that inequality. If every five-coordinate projection of a length-L code is injective, its minimum Hamming distance is at least L−4, allowing one error in seven or two errors in nine.',
'The pigeonhole principle proves the lower bound by comparing M states with at most bᵏ output words. For a collision between states x and x′, write r_j=Aʲx mod M and r′_j=Aʲx′ mod M. Equal outputs mean r_j−r′_j is a multiple of b, while r_j−r′_j≡Aʲ(x−x′) mod M and |r_j−r′_j|<M. Thus collision differences lie in an explicitly defined integer lattice intersected with a bounded box; additionally they must be realizable by a common pair of states. Proving that there is no nonzero bounded lattice vector is sufficient for injectivity, while finding such a vector alone need not give a realizable collision. For the coding statement, two distinct codewords can agree in at most four positions; otherwise an injective five-coordinate projection would coincide. Their distance is therefore at least L−4. Disjoint Hamming balls of radius e follow when 2e is less than that distance.',
'Independently enumerate M=31, A=3, b=8 as a small chosen analogue.',
'The one-, two- and three-output codes have respectively 8,24 and31 distinct words across all31 states. Thus three consecutive outputs are exactly sufficient in this model. Its length-five code has minimum Hamming distance three, hence corrects one arbitrary word error. These statements are checked by exhaustive exact integer enumeration.',
'The actual recurrence and its four-output obstruction are explicit; the small model demonstrates the logic, while the independent lattice supplement supplies the large raw-state theorem.',
'The main entry’s toy enumeration is not a proof of the large raw-state theorem; the separate exact126-pattern lattice supplement supplies that proof. The six-accepted result remains a prior assistant claim because no rejection rule is recovered. No cryptographic security or provenance conclusion follows from the small analogue.',
'Extend the supplied raw-state lattice certificate to accepted observations only after recovering the acceptance rule; model the variable-length emission transducer explicitly, characterize its first-emitting-state quotient, and distinguish genuine hidden-state collisions from unobserved pre-call histories.',
[])
add('Cancellation controls the condition number of an exact sum','kappa_{\\rm sum}','2026-10-16','A','Numerical conditioning',
'How can a mathematically exact identity be numerically unstable, and which error bound detects that instability?',
'The retrieved condition number concerns the sum of terms, rather than the intrinsic truth of the identity. A direct perturbation bound makes its scope precise. It also explains why common-mode symbolic cancellation should occur before evaluating large nearly opposite quantities.',
['Let S=Σ_(j=1)^n t_j≠0 be a real or complex sum. Define κ_sum=Σ|t_j|/|S|.','Assume perturbed terms satisfy |Δt_j|≤ε|t_j|; this is an input relative-error model, separate from the additional error of the summation algorithm.'],
'Sharp first-order relative perturbation bound',
'|ΔS|/|S|≤εκ_sum. For real terms the bound can be attained by suitable independent signed perturbations. If S=0, relative conditioning is undefined and an absolute error bound must be used instead.',
'The triangle inequality gives |ΣΔt_j|≤Σε|t_j|, proving the bound. For real terms, choose every Δt_j with the same sign, of magnitude ε|t_j|; then their sum has magnitude εΣ|t_j|, giving equality. Since |Σt_j|≤Σ|t_j|, κ_sum≥1. For complex terms the same upper bound holds and alignment of perturbation directions similarly attains it under the stated independent complex-error balls. This is a worst-case conditioning theorem; a particular floating-point algorithm can add further rounding errors, while correlated input errors may cancel and require a joint model. Therefore a large κ is a warning about sensitivity, not a proof that any computed value is wrong.',
'Compare an unstable difference with an algebraically simplified one.',
'For t₁=10¹²+1 and t₂=−10¹², S=1 and κ_sum=2·10¹²+1. Independent relative errors of only 10⁻¹² can therefore create absolute errors around two, larger than the true result. If both terms come from the exact common expression c, computing (c+1)−c symbolically as one removes the common uncertainty before numeric evaluation. This is valid only when the two c occurrences really represent the same quantity.',
'The condition number quantifies precisely how cancellation can magnify independent term errors.',
'It is not an a posteriori interval certificate and does not bound errors in unrecovered historical high-precision calculations. Correlations, exact rational arithmetic and compensated summation alter the appropriate error model.',
'For a supplied special-function identity, derive interval enclosures for its individual terms, simplify shared algebraic pieces, and bound the final absolute residual with directed error control before interpreting extra decimal digits.',
[('Higham: accuracy and stability of numerical algorithms','https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9780898718027')])
